# a binding in a nested block does not shadow an enclosing one, it overwrites
# it: a function gets one slot per name, so the inner binding writes the outer
# one's storage and the outer read after the block answers with the inner
# value, or with garbage when the two have different types. it was a wrong
# answer with no diagnostic, so the name is rejected instead.
fn main() -> Int:
    b := 42
    if true:
        b := "inner"
        print("{b}")
    print("{b}")
    return 0
